cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Mathematical Systems: formal definitions of "system," and the maps between them
message: >-
  Cite the Atlas when you rely on a transcription, a provenance grade, or a
  mapping claim. Cite systems-science-foundations separately when the Lean
  formalisation is what you are relying on — the two are different artifacts
  with different warrants, and conflating them overstates both.
type: dataset
authors:
  - given-names: Shingai
    family-names: Thornton
    affiliation: Halcyonic Systems
    orcid: https://orcid.org/0009-0001-8261-1109
abstract: >-
  An auditable catalogue of formal mathematical definitions of "system" drawn
  from the systems theory and systems science traditions. Each entry carries the
  definition transcribed verbatim from its primary source, the location it was
  taken from, the terms it uses as primitives, the examples its author admits
  and refuses, and an evidence code recording how the encoding was established.
  Entries are aligned to BFO/CCO at the annotation layer only, deliberately
  leaving unasserted what each definition is *about*, so that realist and
  constructivist traditions can be catalogued together without the catalogue
  taking a side. Relations between definitions — what one encompasses, what
  survives translation into another, and what is lost — are stated in a mapping
  layer that owes a witness for every claim of loss. Transcriptions are verified
  against primary texts at build time, and entries link to their dependency
  quivers in a companion Lean 4 development.
keywords:
  - systems science
  - systems theory
  - formal ontology
  - definitions
  - Klir
  - Bunge
  - BFO
  - Common Core Ontologies
  - Lean 4
license: CC-BY-4.0
license-url: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

references:
  - type: software
    title: >-
      systems-science-foundations: a Lean 4 formalization of the
      Klir-Bunge-Mobus common core
    authors:
      - given-names: Shingai
        family-names: Thornton
        affiliation: Halcyonic Systems
    repository-code: https://github.com/halcyonic-systems/systems-science-foundations
    notes: >-
      The formal counterpart. Entries carry an `atlas:formalisedAs` pointer to a
      shape category there. That pointer is bibliographic: it records where a
      formalisation lives, not that the formalisation is faithful to the passage.
      Faithfulness is a mapping-layer claim and carries its own evidence.
  - type: generic
    title: Basic Formal Ontology and the Common Core Ontologies
    notes: >-
      Vendored under atlas/imports/ and used for annotation-layer alignment only.
      Both are CC BY 4.0; see THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md for attribution.
